{"answer":"**You set it — it's not den's.** `host.gpu` is a *freeform attribute on the host entity*, and host entities are user data. The `host` argument an aspect receives is the fully resolved host entity from your own flake, so `gpu` only exists there if someone (you, your team, a shared aspect's schema) put it on the host declaration.

**Where it lives:** in your config repo, in the file that declares `den.hosts` (in den's own template that's `templates/default/modules/hosts.nix`; in your repo, wherever your hosts file is):

```nix
den.hosts.x86_64-linux.igloo = {
  gpu = "nvidia";      # freeform — no option declaration needed
  datacenter = "eu-west";
};
```

If you want the attribute on *every* host with a default, declare it once in the kind's schema instead:

```nix
den.schema.host = { options.gpu = lib.mkOption { default = null; type = lib.types.str; }; };
```

That's exactly the pattern from den's docs (`docs/src/content/docs/explanation/entities.mdx`, "Freeform attributes"): hosts, users and homes use `freeformType`, so arbitrary data you attach to an entity is reachable in aspects through the entity's context argument — `{ host, ... }: lib.optionalAttrs (host ? gpu) { ... }`.

**How the value gets to the aspect (verified in `sini/den` @ `main`):**
- `nix/lib/entities/host.nix` — `den.hosts` is `attrsOf (submodule { freeformType = deepMergeAttrs })` and the host instance type is `mkInstanceType den.schema.host { strict = false; … }`, so any attribute on a host instance (like `gpu`) is kept, not rejected.
- `nix/lib/entities/_types.nix` — `resolvedCtxModule` builds `config.resolved = den.lib.resolveEntity kind (ctx // { ${kind} = config })`, i.e. the *whole* host config becomes the `host` context value.
- `nix/lib/resolve-entity.nix` — `entity = ctx.${name}` and `scopeHandlers = constantHandler augmentedCtx`, so the aspect's `host` is that resolved entity, freeform attributes included. Nothing in den populates `gpu`; it just threads your declaration through.

**One gotcha:** if your flake imports `inputs.den.flakeModules.strict`, freeform is off — `gpu` must then be a declared option (via `den.schema.host`), otherwise the host declaration throws a `STRICT MODE` error naming the missing option.

So: grep your own repo for `den.hosts` / `den.schema.host` — `gpu = …` has to be there for `host ? gpu` to be true. If it isn't set anywhere, the `optionalAttrs` block simply never fires (and that's the intended use: the shape of the data is the condition).","confidence":0.85,"gaps":["Did not inspect the asker's own flake/repo to confirm where they actually set (or fail to set) gpu — only verified the den-side mechanism","No eval run: clone is unavailable in this environment, so the flow was verified by reading source (sini/den @ main: nix/lib/entities/host.nix, _types.nix, resolve-entity.nix) and the docs, not by evaluating an attribute","Read the sini/den fork at main (docs link upstream denful/den files of the same name); did not diff against upstream to confirm identical content"],"escalate_forced":"no"}

{"answer":"Because `instantiate` is not an aspect key — it's an option on the **host entity** (`den.hosts.<system>.<name>.instantiate`). Setting it on `den.aspects.myhost` puts a freeform key named `instantiate` in the aspect, and the pipeline never uses that to build the output.

**What happens to your aspect key** (all from `sini/den` @ `main`, which is a fork of `denful/den`):
- The pipeline classifies aspect keys in `nix/lib/aspects/fx/key-classification.nix`. `instantiate` is in neither `builtinStructuralKeys` (name/description/meta/includes/excludes/provides/policies/…) nor `den.classes` (nixos, darwin, homeManager, …) nor `den.quirks`, so it lands in `unregisteredClassKeys`.
- `nix/lib/aspects/fx/handlers/classify.nix` then folds those into `classKeys`, and `emit-classes.nix` collects the value into a class bucket literally named `"instantiate"`. No output consumes that bucket — the host's module set is taken only from the `nixos`/`darwin` bucket (`perHostProjection` in `nix/lib/aspects/fx/resolve.nix` reads `materialized.${hostClass}`). So your aspect's `instantiate` is silently dropped.

**What actually builds your host**:
- `modules/policies/flake.nix` → `den.policies.system-to-os-outputs` iterates over `den.hosts.${system}` and registers `den.lib.policy.instantiate <entity record>` for each host.
- Post-pipeline, `applyInstantiates` in `nix/lib/aspects/fx/resolve.nix` calls `entry.spec.instantiate (mkArgs entry.spec)`, where `spec` is that **entity record**. The entity's `instantiate` option is defined in `nix/lib/entities/host.nix`, and its default is `inputs.nixpkgs.lib.nixosSystem` — i.e. your unpatched nixpkgs. That's what runs, and nothing in your aspect can override it.

**The fix** — set it on the host entity, not the aspect:
```nix
den.hosts."x86_64-linux".myhost = {
  # args passed are { modules = [...]; } (plus pkgs/system only if you set
  # those options on the same host entity)
  instantiate = { modules, ... }:
    inputs.nixpkgs-patched.lib.nixosSystem {
      inherit modules;
      specialArgs = { pkgs = inputs.nixpkgs-patched.legacyPackages."x86_64-linux"; };
    };
  # ...
};
```
The option's own description in `host.nix` says exactly this: "Set explicitly if you need: a custom input name, eg, nixos-unstable; adding specialArgs when absolutely required." Keep everything else in your aspect as-is — it still supplies the `nixos` class content that flows into `modules`.

Note: the same split applies to `darwin` (`inputs.darwin.lib.darwinSystem`) and `systemManager` hosts.","confidence":0.85,"gaps":["Did not evaluate the asker's actual flake (no repo/rev given) to confirm their host is declared under den.hosts and that only the entity-level instantiate applies","Source read from sini/den main branch (fork of denful/den); the asker's checked-out den rev may differ slightly (e.g. if they use an older release where key classification differs)"],"escalate_forced":"no"}

