{"answer":"Use a flat-form `homeManager` class module that asks for `host` from the den context and gates the content with `lib.mkIf`:

```nix
# den.aspects.my-feature: homeManager content only where the host has the capability
den.aspects.my-feature = {
  homeManager = { host, pkgs, ... }:
    lib.mkIf (builtins.elem "graphics" (host.capabilities or [ ])) {
      # your home-manager options here
      home.packages = [ pkgs.egl-info ];
    };
};
```

Then activate it **at user scope**, because homeManager content is only forwarded from the user's resolution tree into `home-manager.users.<userName>`:

```nix
# every user:
den.schema.user.includes = [ den.aspects.my-feature ];
# or just one:
den.aspects.alice.includes = [ den.aspects.my-feature ];
```

Your hosts keep their one-line capability declarations as freeform metadata (e.g. `den.hosts.x86_64-linux.laptop.capabilities = [ "graphics" "battery" ]`); no `enable` flag anywhere — the `mkIf` is the only condition.

How this works and what I checked (denful/den @ main):

- **`host` is available inside class modules.** The flat-form class module mechanism inspects `builtins.functionArgs`, pre-applies any arg that is in the den context, and leaves the rest (`pkgs`, `config`, ...) to the module system — `docs/src/content/docs/explanation/class-modules.mdx` ("Context args in class modules", "How It Works"). The user's context carries its host: `nix/lib/entities/host.nix` sets `config._module.args.host = host` on the user type, so `host.capabilities` is exactly what you declared on the host.
- **Skips cleanly where there is no user.** A class module that requests an entity arg not in the current context is skipped with a `lib.warn`, not an error (class-modules.mdx, "Unsatisfied args") — so the same aspect can carry a `nixos = { host, ... }` module for host scope and a `homeManager = { host, ... }` module that only bites at user scope.
