function.arguments:
{"answer":"Yes — a host-profile aspect can own the user roster. In den, a host's users are defined by exactly one thing: the `users` option of that host's module. The built-in `den.policies.host-to-users` fans out over `lib.attrValues host.users` and nothing else (`modules/policies/core.nix`, activated via `den.schema.host.includes`). So the question reduces to: how does your profile aspect feed the host module's `users` option. There are two clean ways, plus a policy-based variant.

**Option A — set `config.users` inside the profile aspect's OS-class module (no schema changes).**
The host module's `mainModule` is `{ inherit (config.__resolveResult) imports; }`, where `__resolveResult = den.lib.aspects.resolveWithPaths config.class config.resolved` and `resolved = den.lib.resolveEntity "host" ctx` (which self-provides `host.aspect`) — all in `nix/lib/entities/host.nix`. That means every class module of a host's resolved aspect is an *import of the host's own module system*, the same one that owns the `users` option (`users = lib.mkOption { default = { }; type = attrsOf (userType config); }`, same file). So a module in your profile aspect can set it directly:

```nix
# one profile = one aspect, defined once
den.aspects.workstation = {
  nixos = {
    # den-level roster: every host using this profile gets these users
    config.users = {
      alice = { };
      bob = { };
    };
    # ...your normal host OS config...
  };

  # per-user config: entity-named keys on the aspect are automatically
  # included into each matching user's scope (core.nix: hasChild/include)
  alice = { homeManager = { ... }; };
  bob   = { homeManager = { ... }; };
};

# hosts become one-liners
den.hosts.x86_64-linux = {
  laptop  = { aspect = den.aspects.workstation; };
  desktop = { aspect = den.aspects.workstation; };
};
```

Notes: `aspect` is a raw option whose default is `den.aspects.<hostName>` (`lookupAspect` in `entities/_types.nix`), so a host whose *name* matches the profile name gets it with zero config; name your host differently and set `aspect` explicitly as above. User entities only exist as `host.users.<name>`, so the roster lives on the host entity even when the *definition* comes from the aspect. Remember the OS accounts themselves need the opt-in `den.batteries.define-user` (e.g. in each user aspect's `includes`, as the template's `tux.nix` does) — that's separate from the den-level association.

**Option B — keep the roster as data on the aspect, apply it with one schema line.**
If you'd rather keep `users` as plain readable data on the profile and not bury it in an OS module:

```nix
den.aspects.workstation = {
  users = { alice = { }; bob = { }; };   # freeform data on the aspect
  nixos = { ... };
  alice = { ... }; bob = { ... };        # per-user config as above
};

# applied to all hosts (den.schema.host is a module imported into every
# host — same pattern the microvm template uses: den.schema.host.imports = [...])
den.schema.host = { config, ... }: {
  config.users = config.aspect.users or { };
};
```

This is the docs' recommended home for "features of all hosts" (`declare-hosts.mdx`, "Base Modules").

**From a policy.** A policy can drive the *fan-out* but cannot create user entities that aren't already in `host.users` — user entities only come from that module option, and `host-to-users` only iterates it. If you want conditional/profile-driven routing, write a policy that resolves users by name:

```nix
den.policies.profile-users = { host, ... }:
  lib.concatMap (
    u: [ (den.lib.policy.resolve.shared { user = host.users.${u}; }) ]
  ) (host.profiles or [ ]);   # host.profiles = [ "alice" "bob" ] (freeform)

den.schema.host.includes = [ den.policies.profile-users ];
```

This mirrors `host-to-users`'s own `resolve.shared { inherit user; }` (`modules/policies/core.nix`). The user definitions themselves still have to exist in `host.users` (via Option A/B), the policy just selects which ones fan out — useful for "this host gets a subset of the profile's users".

**How I know:** source reads of `sini/den@main` (fork of `denful/den`): `modules/policies/core.nix` (host-to-users + entity-named-key include), `nix/lib/entities/host.nix` (users option, mainModule → resolveWithPaths → resolveEntity), `nix/lib/entities/_types.nix` (lookupAspect default), `templates/default/modules/{hosts,tux,igloo,defaults}.nix` (the per-host `users.tux = {}` pattern you're removing), `templates/microvm/modules/microvm-integration.nix` (schema.host extension pattern), and the docs (`declare-hosts.mdx`, `reference/policies.mdx`, `reference/batteries.mdx`).

Caveat: I verified the import path (aspect class modules → host module system → `users` option) from source but did not run a live eval of an aspect-set `config.users`, since the den repo's root flake doesn't contain the template hosts and your repo wasn't part of this thread — if Option A misbehaves on your rev, that's the thing to check.","confidence":0.75,"gaps":["Did not live-eval a config where an aspect's nixos module sets host `config.users` (den root flake has no template hosts; clone unavailable this run) — Option A is source-derived from mainModule/resolveWithPaths in nix/lib/entities/host.nix","Answer is against sini/den@main (denful/den fork); asker's den rev unknown","Merge behavior when two aspects define the same user in `users` (attrsOf per-key last-wins) not verified"],"escalate_forced":"no"}

