{"answer":"You do it in two parts: put the `homeManager` class on each user of that host, then deliver the shared option from the **host's** aspect via `provides.to-users` — that's the built-in cross-entity route that spans every user on the host.

```nix
# 1. Each user on igloo gets a Home Manager environment
den.hosts.x86_64-linux.igloo = {
  users.alice.classes = [ "homeManager" ];
  users.bob.classes   = [ "homeManager" ];
};

# 2. One shared HM option, delivered to all of igloo's users
den.aspects.igloo = {
  provides.to-users = { user, ... }: {
    homeManager.home.packages = [ pkgs.htop ]; # any homeManager option
  };
};
```

How it works (from den's source, `sini/den` @ `main`):

- The `home-manager` battery (`modules/aspects/batteries/home-manager.nix`) registers a `host-to-hm-users` policy built by `makeHomeEnv` in `nix/lib/home-env.nix`. When the host has at least one user with `homeManager` in `classes`, it imports the Home Manager OS module (`home-manager.enable` defaults to exactly that check, `host-has-user-with-class`) and, for every such user, forwards that user's resolved aspect `homeManager` class into `home-manager.users.<userName>` on the host (`forwardPathFn` = `[ "home-manager" "users" user.userName ]`).
- The `provides.to-users` mechanism is documented in `docs/src/content/docs/guides/mutual.mdx`: "To configure several users — or to select per-user — register the `provides` on the **host** aspect, whose subtree spans every user on it." The doc's own example is exactly this shape: `provides.to-users = { user, ... }: { homeManager.programs.vim.enable = user.name != "tux"; }`. You can also narrow per user inside the function (e.g. `user.name == "alice"`), and `provides.alice.homeManager.<opt> = ...` for a single named user.

Two things to avoid / note:

- Don't rely on a host-scope parametric aspect `den.aspects.igloo = { user, ... }: { homeManager = ...; }`. Per `guides/home-manager.mdx`, earlier releases let that leak home content to users; now a host-scope parametric aspect emits `homeManager` class-locally **on the host**, where it is inert — so it never reaches the users' Home Manager evaluation. Route it through `provides.to-users` (or a `to-users` policy / the host-aspects battery).
