{"answer":"Pass the value through the **`_module.args`** option. That is the supported way to make custom values available as arguments to module functions in a `lib.evalModules` evaluation:

```nix
{ lib, ... }:
let
  myValue = { greeting = "hello"; };
in
lib.evalModules {
  modules = [
    # Set the argument; every module in this evaluation can see it
    {
      config._module.args.myValue = myValue;
    }

    # Consume it: a module that is a function gets its named parameters
    # resolved against config._module.args when they aren't top-level module args
    { myValue, ... }: {
      config.greeting = myValue.greeting;
    }
  ];
}
```

You don't need to declare `options.myValue` for this — `_module.args` is an internal option of type `types.lazyAttrsOf types.raw`, so it accepts any value lazily (heavy values won't be evaluated until actually used).

How this works, from nixpkgs `lib/modules.nix` (master, `lib.evalModules`):

- The `evalModules` binding declares the internal option: `options._module.args = mkOption { type = types.lazyAttrsOf types.raw; ... }`, described as "Additional arguments passed to each module in addition to ones like `lib`, `config`, and `pkgs`, `modulesPath`".
- When a module is a function, `applyModuleArgs` builds the argument set: for each named parameter of the module function (`functionArgs f`), it uses `args.<name>` if provided at the top level, **otherwise falls back to `config._module.args.<name>`** (the source even carries the error context: "noting that argument `<name>` is not externally provided, so querying `_module.args` instead, requiring `config`").
- So: set `config._module.args.<name>` in one module (or any module), and any module function in the same `evalModules` call can destructure `{ <name>, ... }` or read `config._module.args.<name>` directly.

Two things to avoid/keep in mind:

1. **The `args` argument of `evalModules` is deprecated.** `lib.evalModules { args.myValue = ...; modules = [...]; }` still works (it's merged into `config._module.args` internally), but current master warns: `"The args argument to evalModules is deprecated. Please set config._module.args instead."`
2. **`specialArgs` is reserved for import-time values.** The source comment says it "should only be used for special arguments that need to be evaluated when resolving module structure (like in imports). For everything else, there's `_module.args`." Use `specialArgs` only when the value is needed to compute `imports`; everything else goes in `_module.args`.

Caveat: submodule args don't inherit across the submodule boundary (per the option's doc: "Submodules do not inherit args from their parent module") — this is only about the top-level `evalModules` call, which is the usual case.

How I know: I read the full `lib/modules.nix` on NixOS/nixpkgs master (fetched via raw.githubusercontent.com); the cited text is quoted from the `evalModules`, `_module.args` option, and `applyModuleArgs` definitions in that file.","confidence":0.85,"gaps":["Pinned rev of nixpkgs not obtained (GitHub commits API 403, HTML page 429) — source read is from master at fetch time, no SHA cited","Did not run a computed eval of evalModules with a custom arg (the eval tool selects a flake attribute and cannot pass arguments to the function); the mechanism is verified by source read only","Behavior verified on nixpkgs master only; older releases (e.g. 24.05/24.11) not checked for the deprecation warning"],"escalate_forced":"no"}

