{"answer":"Use NixOS **specialisations** — that is exactly what this feature exists for. You keep *one* system configuration in your flake; each `specialisation.<name>` is built as a full NixOS system layered on top of it and gets its **own boot entry**, so you pick the variant at boot.

**How to set it up**

In your flake, keep your normal `nixosConfigurations.laptop`, and add a module with the variants, e.g. `gpu-variants.nix`:

```nix
{ ... }:
{
  specialisation = {
    # Same system, NVIDIA driver
    nvidia = {
      # inheritParentConfig = true is the default: users, services,
      # disk layout etc. are inherited unchanged.
      configuration = {
        hardware.nvidia.enable = true;
        hardware.nvidia.modesetting.enable = true;
        services.xserver.videoDrivers = [ "nvidia" ];
      };
    };

    # Same system, amdgpu driver
    amdgpu = {
      configuration = {
        hardware.amdgpu.enable = true;
        services.xserver.videoDrivers = [ "amdgpu" ];
      };
    };
  };
}
```

Then `sudo nixos-rebuild switch --flake .#laptop` as usual. The boot menu now shows the main entry plus one entry per specialisation — titles like `NixOS 25.05 (nvidia)` / `NixOS 25.05 (amdgpu)`. The main config stays the boot default; the variants are there whenever you want them. (Gist with the full flake example proposed.)

**Useful extras**

- To try a variant *without* changing the boot default: `sudo /run/current-system/specialisation/nvidia/bin/switch-to-configuration test` (this is the example from the option's own documentation). Activating the variant's toplevel with `switch`/`boot` makes its entry the default instead.
- `specialisation.<name>.configuration` is a regular NixOS module: you can `imports`, set any option, including `boot.initrd.kernelParams` if a driver option is needed in the initrd.
- `boot.loader.systemd-boot.configurationLimit` defaults to `null` (no limit), so all non-GC'd generations and their variants stay in the menu; variant entries are garbage-collected together with their parent generation.

**How I know this** (all read from `NixOS/nixpkgs` @ master):

- `nixos/modules/system/activation/specialisation.nix` defines `specialisation` (it's in the base `module-list.nix`), with the comment: *"you can provide an easy way to boot the same configuration as you use, but with another kernel"*, and the doc: *"To switch to a specialised configuration (e.g. `fewJobsManyCores`) at runtime, run: `sudo /run/current-system/specialisation/fewJobsManyCores/bin/switch-to-configuration test`"*. The top-level builder symlinks each child into `$out/specialisation/<name>`.
- `nixos/modules/system/activation/bootspec.nix` embeds every specialisation's *complete* bootspec (its own kernel, initrd, kernel params) into the parent's `boot.json` under `org.nixos.specialisation.v1`.
- `nixos/modules/system/boot/loader/systemd-boot/systemd-boot-builder.py` writes one loader entry per specialisation per generation (`title = "{distro} (name)"`, `version = "Generation N <label>"`), and `write_loader_conf` makes the entry matching the activated toplevel the default.

**Two things to be aware of**

- This needs a nixpkgs release that has specialisation support (all recent ones; I verified master but didn't pin the exact release it landed in). If you're on an old release, the fallback is defining two independent `nixosConfigurations` in your flake and installing each with `nixos-rebuild switch --flake .#<name>` — each becomes its own boot entry.
- Driver-specific details (which of `hardware.nvidia.*` / `hardware.amdgpu.*` / `services.xserver.videoDrivers` / initrd params you actually need) depend on your hardware; the options in the example are the standard ones but I didn't verify them against your specific card.","confidence":0.85,"gaps":["Did not evaluate the asker's own flake (no repo/rev provided); example verified against NixOS/nixpkgs master source only","Exact release in which `specialisation` support was introduced not verified","Two-independent-nixosConfigurations fallback: boot-entry titles/profile behaviour on current master not verified","Per-driver option details (nvidia/amdgpu/initrd requirements) not verified for the asker's hardware"],"escalate_forced":"no"}

