{"answer":"Use the **`virtualisation.vmVariant`** option. Anything you set there is applied only to the system build that `nixos-rebuild build-vm` produces (`system.build.vm`), not to `system.build.toplevel` used by `build`/`switch`/`test`:

```nix
{
  # ... your normal config (used by build/switch) ...

  virtualisation.vmVariant = {
    # Only takes effect in `nixos-rebuild build-vm`
    virtualisation.memorySize = 2048;
    virtualisation.cores = 2;
    virtualisation.forwardPorts = [
      { from = "host"; host.port = 2222; guest.port = 22; }
    ];
    services.openssh.enable = true;
  };
}
```

For `nixos-rebuild build-vm-with-bootloader` the matching option is `virtualisation.vmVariantWithBootLoader`.

**How it works (verified in NixOS/nixpkgs, branch `master`):**

- `nixos/modules/virtualisation/build-vm.nix` declares `virtualisation.vmVariant` as "Machine configuration to be added for the vm script produced by `nixos-rebuild build-vm`", and sets `system.build.vm = lib.mkDefault config.virtualisation.vmVariant.system.build.vm`.
- `virtualisation.vmVariant` is an `extendModules` built on top of `nixos/modules/virtualisation/qemu-vm.nix`. So a `build-vm` build is a *second* evaluation of your config with `qemu-vm.nix` + your `vmVariant` module layered on top. Options in `vmVariant` (plain values, `lib.mkIf`, even `imports`) stay inside that VM evaluation and don't leak into your host build.
- `qemu-vm.nix` defines all the VM options (`virtualisation.memorySize`, `cores`, `forwardPorts`, `sharedDirectories`, `qemu.options`, `useNixStoreImage`, …) and the `system.build.vm` derivation (the `run-<name>-vm` script that launches QEMU).

**Two caveats:**

1. This is a per-build-output mechanism, not runtime detection: there is no config attribute that says "I am currently being built as a VM" inside a single evaluation. `build` and `build-vm` simply build different outputs of the same config.
2. The VM evaluation already auto-adjusts several options for VM use using `mkVMOverride` (`lib/modules.nix`: `mkVMOverride = mkOverride 10`, which beats plain user definitions, priority 100, and even `mkForce`, priority 50): `fileSystems` (replaced with virtiofs mounts), `swapDevices`, `boot.initrd.luks.devices`, `services.xserver.*`, `networking.wireless.enable`, `services.connman.enable`. If you need to override one of those specifically for the VM, use the matching `virtualisation.*` option (e.g. `virtualisation.fileSystems`). Also note you cannot nest `virtualisation.vmVariant` inside `virtualisation.vmVariant` — that throws.

(Side note: on current nixpkgs the `nixos-rebuild` on your system is the new Python implementation `nixos-rebuild-ng`, per `nixos/modules/installer/tools/tools.nix`; `build-vm` targets `system.build.vm` as described above.)","confidence":0.85,"gaps":["Read nixpkgs files at branch `master` via raw.githubusercontent; GitHub API was rate-limited (403) so the citation is not pinned to an exact SHA","Did not read the nixos-rebuild-ng (current Python nixos-rebuild) source to confirm `build-vm` maps to `system.build.vm`; relied on the option description in build-vm.nix which names `nixos-rebuild build-vm`"],"escalate_forced":"no"}

