function.arguments:
{"answer":"## What `evalModuleTree` is

`evalModuleTree` is the main entry point of **gen-merge** (`sini/gen-merge`), a pure-Nix, nixpkgs.lib-free module merge engine — the "merge half" of the pure-gen module system. Per the repo README: it collects a tree of modules, ties the self-referential config fixpoint (one local fix per call; `config._module.args.X = config` lets modules cross-reference), resolves per-option definitions by priority, recurses into structural types, routes undeclared keys through a freeform type, and verifies leaves.

Signature from the README's Usage section:

```
evalModuleTree { specialArgs ? {}; check ? true; prefix ? [];
                 coreShortCircuit ? false; warmFrom ? null;
                 editedModules ? []; class ? null }
  modules → { config; options; type; provenance; undeclared;
              deprecations; _type; class; extendModules; graph }
```

Usage (all four dependencies are required, none defaulted — the flake exposes `lib = import ./. { prelude; types; memo; scope; }` over gen-prelude, gen-types, gen-memo, gen-scope):

```nix
result = evalModuleTree { } [
  { options.name = mkOption { type = t.str; default = "anon"; }; }
  { name = mkForce "pinned"; }
];
result.config   # ⇒ { name = "pinned"; }
```

It is *not* its own fixpoint driver: the module tree is a single node on gen-scope's graph evaluator. Beyond the merged `.config`, the result adds gen's own introspection surface: `.provenance` (which files defined each loc, which won), `.undeclared`, `.deprecations`, `.warmDecision` (a warm/memoized re-eval trace), plus helpers like `bandedLeaves` and `mergeTypes`.

## How it relates to nixpkgs `lib.evalModules`

**It is a drop-in replacement, and nixpkgs is its reference yardstick.** The README's design rule ("byte-mode"): nixpkgs `lib.evalModules` is the reference, and the same modules must yield the same config, byte-for-byte, on the surface a real configuration uses — down to the priority-resolved winner at every option. Concretely:

- **Scope:** it replaces `lib.evalModules` + lib.types-merge, implementing the priority subset the den/gen corpus actually uses: `mkDefault`/`mkForce`/`mkMerge`/`mkIf` (lowest priority-number wins, ties merge; equal-priority defs merge in reverse module order, byte-identical to nixpkgs — e.g. three modules contributing `[a] [b] [c]` merge to `[c b a]`), plus `disabledModules`, per-key name/`_module.args` bindings, and the nixpkgs order pass (`mkBefore`/`mkAfter`, i.e. `sortProperties`).
- **Works in both directions with nixpkgs:**
  - Every type in `genMerge.types` carries the full 14-field nixpkgs `mkOptionType` protocol shape, so the *same type value serves both engines* — that's how gen-schema injects gen-merge-typed options into a submodule that a real nixpkgs `lib.evalModules` evaluates (the flake-parts corpus path).
  - A result's `.type` is an option type named `submodule` that a real nixpkgs `lib.evalModules` mounts bare, so a gen tree can nest inside a nixpkgs evaluation (and vice versa via the types namespace).
  - nixpkgs is pulled **only in CI** (per `flake.nix`, confirmed from the repo) as the equivalence oracle: parity cells run the same merge through both engines and assert against literal values.
- **Declared divergences** (from the README's "Known byte-mode boundaries" and surrounding sections), so it is not byte-identical *everywhere*:
  - nixpkgs injects `lib` at every `evalModules` level; gen-merge injects none — a module reading `lib` is refused by name (`module argument 'lib' is not defined`) until the caller threads it via `specialArgs`.
  - Redeclarations: nixpkgs refuses outright when both declarations carry `default`/`example`/`description`/`apply` ("already declared"); gen-merge right-biases the non-type fields (later declaration wins) and keeps the shadowed declaration reachable in `options.<o>.overridden`.
  - `mergeDefaultOption`'s multi-function arm: nixpkgs passes raw values into `getValues` (uncatchable abort, or a stray unwrap); gen-merge re-enters with each definition's value already applied (`[ (x: [x]) (x: [x+1]) ]` applied to 1 ⇒ `[ 1 2 ]`).
  - gen-merge adds a `check = false` door nixpkgs lacks (undeclared keys reported in `.undeclared` instead of thrown), and its provenance forcing contract is stronger than nixpkgs' `definitionsWithLocations` (reading `winners` forces each winning def's value to WHNF).

## How I know

`clone` and `eval` were unavailable in this session, so I verified against the repo's own documentation, not an eval: I fetched `https://github.com/sini/gen-merge` (README, main) and `flake.nix` from raw.githubusercontent.com at main (latest commit `03f17a6`, Oct 16 2026 per the commits page). All claims above are quoted from the README sections I read (Usage, "The 7-item merge primitive", "The priority subset", "The nixpkgs optionType protocol", "Redeclaring an option", "Known byte-mode boundaries") and from the flake's `outputs.lib` wiring.","confidence":0.8,"gaps":["Did not evaluate lib.evalModuleTree at rev 03f17a6 (eval tool unavailable in this session); the usage example is from the README, not an eval","Did not read lib/modules.nix with line numbers — all behavioural claims are from the repo README (main) and flake.nix, not independently checked source lines"],"escalate_forced":"no"}

