{"answer":"Yes — den is explicitly built for this, and den's own repo ships a working example of exactly your shape (no hosts, custom class only): the `nvf-standalone` template. There are two ways to do it.

**1. As a pure library: `den.lib.aspects.resolve <class> <aspect>`**

`den.lib.aspects.resolve` (documented in `docs/src/content/docs/reference/lib.mdx`) resolves an aspect for one class and returns a module: in `nix/lib/aspects/fx/resolve.nix` it is defined as

```nix
fxResolve = mkPipeline: args: { inherit (fxResolveFull mkPipeline args) imports; };
```

i.e. it returns `{ imports = [ ... ]; }` — the collected class modules from the whole aspect tree (`includes`, `provides`, forward batteries), with den context args already partially applied where a context exists. You then evaluate the list yourself with your own module system. This is exactly what den's own template does in `templates/nvf-standalone/modules/nvf-integration.nix`:

```nix
module = den.lib.aspects.resolve "nvf" aspect;   # { imports = [...]; }
...
(inputs.nvf.lib.neovimConfiguration { modules = [ module ]; }).neovim;  # user's own eval
```

So your version would look like:

```nix
myEval = pkgs:
  let
    collected = den.lib.aspects.resolve "myclass" den.aspects.root;
  in
  (lib.evalModules {
    modules = [ your-options collected ];  # collected is { imports = [...]; }
    specialArgs = { inherit pkgs; };
  }).config;
```

There's also `den.lib.aspects.resolveImports` (same, but skips entity instantiation — for nested extraction), and `den.lib.resolveEntity <kind> <ctx>` if you want the aspect driven by an entity record: the docs' own example is `den.lib.aspects.resolve "flake" (den.lib.resolveEntity "flake" { })`.

**2. Through den's pipeline with zero hosts**

`templates/nvf-standalone` runs `inputs.den.flakeModule` with **no `den.hosts` at all** — it hooks the aspect into a custom entity kind and lets den collect/evaluate:

```nix
# templates/nvf-standalone/modules/den.nix
den.schema.flake-system.includes = [ den.aspects.flake ];
den.aspects.flake.packages = { pkgs, ... }: { my-neovim = ...; };
```

and its flake.nix is just `nixpkgs.lib.evalModules { modules = [ ... ]; }.config.flake` — den's flake policies (`modules/policies/flake.nix`) register `packages`/`apps`/`checks`/`devShells`/`legacyPackages` as classes and route them into `config.flake.<output>.<system>` with `pkgs = inputs.nixpkgs.legacyPackages.<system>` attached. If your custom class has an arbitrary name, register it in `den.classes.<name>` (see `modules/options.nix`, and the "Custom Nix Classes" doc: unregistered freeform aspect keys are treated as nested aspects, not classes) and route it into a target class with `den.lib.policy.route`, or use `den.batteries.forward` — both per `docs/.../guides/custom-classes.mdx`.

**Caveats**

- In a hosts-less setup, class module functions that ask for `{ host, ... }` (or `user`) get the warning "class module requests 'host' but no host context is available" and are left unwrapped (`nix/lib/aspects/fx/class-module.nix`). Keep modules to `{ pkgs, ... }`-style args, or supply the context via `resolveEntity`/`__scopeHandlers`.
- The reference doc labels `den.lib.aspects.resolve` "Internal", but the bundled `nvf-standalone` template uses it as a library API from user code, so it is the established way to do what you're asking.

Verified from source at `sini/den` `main` (a fork of `denful/den`); I read the files via raw GitHub (clone was unavailable in this thread) and did not evaluate your own configuration — no repo/rev was given to check against.","confidence":0.8,"gaps":["Did not run an eval: no repo/rev of the asker's config was provided, and clone was unavailable in this thread, so all claims are from source reads of sini/den@main via raw.githubusercontent.com","Did not verify against denful/den main directly (sini/den is a fork; may have drifted)","The exact route wiring for an arbitrarily-named custom class into a flake output (den.classes + policy.route) was taken from the docs pattern, not eval'd end-to-end"],"escalate_forced":"no"}

