den.quirks.nixpkgs-overlays.description = "nixpkgs overlays collected from aspects";

# the rust aspect contributes its overlay — note the `_: [ … ]` wrapper
den.aspects.rust.nixpkgs-overlays = _: [ inputs.rust-overlay.overlays.default ];

# one consumer per class applies whatever was collected
den.aspects.nixpkgs-overlays = {
  os = { nixpkgs-overlays, lib, ... }: {
    nixpkgs.overlays = lib.unique nixpkgs-overlays;
  };
  homeManager = { nixpkgs-overlays, lib, ... }: {
    nixpkgs.overlays = lib.unique nixpkgs-overlays;
  };
};
den.default.includes = [ den.aspects.nixpkgs-overlays ];
```

Two gotchas the docs call out:
- **Wrap the list in a function: `_: [ overlay ]`.** Den calls a function-valued quirk entry with the scope's context. An overlay is itself a function, so a bare `nixpkgs-overlays = [ overlay ];` gets called with the context and evaluation dies inside nixpkgs with `attempt to call something which is not a function but a set`.
- **`useGlobalPkgs` changes where overlays live.** By default each home builds its own pkgs and the `homeManager` class applies its own overlays. If a host sets `home-manager.useGlobalPkgs = true`, Home Manager ignores the home's `nixpkgs.overlays` (with a warning) and the overlays for home packages must go on the host's `os`/`nixos` class instead (the docs show a `pipe.expose` policy that projects user-contributed overlays up to the host in that case).

Sources: den's own docs on `denful/den` main — `docs/src/content/docs/guides/nixpkgs.mdx` (Overlays/Channels guide) and `quirk-recipes.mdx` (overlay quirk recipe); the repo notes each den-specific behaviour on those pages was checked with a den CI test before being written down.

One caveat: I verified this against the upstream `denful/den` docs/source. `eval` and `clone` were unavailable in this environment, so I could not evaluate your specific config — if you share your flake's repo/rev I can check the exact attribute values.

