{"answer":"Both move a custom class's content into another class — but they live in different layers and have different shapes.

**`den.batteries.forward`** — an *aspect factory* (a battery; also reachable as `den.provides.forward` / `den._.forward`). You call it with a spec and **include the returned aspect** for the new class to exist (`modules/aspects/batteries/forward.nix`: "An aspect that imports all modules defined for `from` class into a target `into` submodule"). Its spec fields are per-item functions over `each`: `{ each, fromClass, intoClass, intoPath, fromAspect, guard?, adaptArgs?, adapterModule? }` (`nix/lib/forward.nix`), so one call can fan out over many entities (e.g. every user on a host). This is how den implements the `homeManager`, `hjem` and `maid` classes (`nix/lib/home-env.nix` uses `den.batteries.forward` per user).

**`den.lib.policy.route`** — a *policy effect*, a constructor under `den.lib.policy` used inside a policy function (`ctx -> [ effects ]`) that you register in `den.policies` and activate via an `includes` list (`den.default.includes`, `den.schema.<kind>.includes`, or an aspect's `includes`). It declares one delivery edge: collect the content of a class (or quirk) and move it into a target class at an attrpath — `{ fromClass, intoClass, path | intoPath, reinstantiate?, guard?, adaptArgs? }`. In `nix/lib/policy-effects.nix` it is a permanent shim over `deliver` (mode: `merge` at the class root, `nest` at a non-empty path, `verbatim` for `reinstantiate = true`), and the comment there says it "replaces `den.batteries.forward` for the common case". Den's own built-in `user`, `os` and `wsl` classes use it — e.g. `modules/aspects/batteries/os-user.nix` registers the `user` class in `den.classes` and routes it with `den.lib.policy.route { fromClass = "user"; intoClass = host.class; path = [ "users" "users" user.userName ]; }` in the `user-to-host` policy.

**Key differences**

| | `den.batteries.forward` | `den.lib.policy.route` |
|---|---|---|
| Kind | aspect (included in `includes`) | policy effect (returned by a policy in `den.policies`) |
| Creates the custom class | yes — the included aspect *is* the class | no — you register it yourself: `den.classes.<name> = { }` (+ optional `forwardTo`, `parentPath`, `parentArg`) |
| Fan-out | per-item `each` + per-item `fromAspect`/`intoPath` in one expression | one edge per effect; policies fire per entity context, so you map over your entities in the policy fn |
| Extras | `adapterModule` (custom module type for the forwarded submodule), `fromCtx`/`fromAspect` control, `evalConfig` | `reinstantiate` (verbatim delivery), quirk content routing, composes with `policy.for` / `policy.when` / `excludes` |

**Which to choose** — den's own "Choosing a Mechanism" doc (docs/src/content/docs/explanation/choosing-a-mechanism.mdx) says it plainly: "`forward` is the older battery with more options in one call; `policy.route` is the policy effect it is built from. Den's own `os`, `user` and `wsl` classes use `route`. **Reach for `route` in new code** and for `forward` when you need one of its extras (per-item `each`, adapter modules) in a single expression." Concretely:

- **New code, common case → `route`.** Register the class and add a policy, e.g.:
  ```nix
  { den, ... }:
  {
    den.classes.files = { };
    den.policies.files-to-flake-parts = _: [
      (den.lib.policy.route {
        fromClass = "files";
        intoClass = "flake-parts";
        path = [ "files" ];
      })
    ];
    den.schema.flake-parts.includes = [ den.policies.files-to-flake-parts ];
  }
  ```
- **Use `forward`** when you want the ergonomic class-factory shape — e.g. forwarding a *list* of entities where each needs its own `fromAspect`/`intoPath` (the homeManager/hjem/maid pattern), or you need `adapterModule` to set the forwarded submodule's type.","confidence":0.9,"gaps":["No eval was run: clone is unavailable in this environment, so all source was read from denful/den's main branch via the web (rev not pinned) and not evaluated","Behaviour at the asker's specific den release/version not verified — API details may differ on older releases"],"escalate_forced":"no"}

