{"answer":"Make the feature aspect a **user-scope parametric aspect** and gate its `homeManager` attribute with a plain condition on the host's capability data:

```nix
den.aspects.<feature> = { host, user, ... }:
  lib.optionalAttrs (host.capabilities ? graphics) {
    homeManager = { pkgs, ... }: {
      home.packages = [ pkgs.<your-gui-app> ];
    };
  };
```

How each piece works, from `denful/den`@main:

1. **`capabilities` is freeform host data.** The host schema (`nix/lib/entities/host.nix`) has no built-in `capabilities` option — hosts are open (`freeformType`) submodules, so `den.hosts.x86_64-linux.laptop.capabilities = { graphics = true; }` is a plain attribute readable anywhere `host` is in scope. If yours is a list (`[ "graphics" "battery" ]`), gate with `lib.elem "graphics" (host.capabilities or [ ])` instead.

2. **`{ host, user, ... }` activates only where both exist.** Den dispatches aspects by argument shape (docs: `explanation/parametric.mdx`), and at user scope `user`'s `host` is bound to the enclosing host (`entities/host.nix`: the user type's `options.host = { default = host; }`).

3. **The `homeManager` content then reaches the user's home.** The home-manager battery (`nix/lib/home-env.nix`, `modules/aspects/batteries/home-manager.nix`) runs a user-scope policy that forwards each user's `homeManager` class into `home-manager.users.<userName>` — for users whose `classes` include `"homeManager"`. On hosts without the capability, `optionalAttrs` drops the whole `homeManager` attribute, so nothing is forwarded, while the aspect's other classes (e.g. `nixos`) are unaffected. No `mkIf` clutter — the condition is just data.

**One trap:** include the aspect at *user* scope, not host scope. A parametric aspect included at host scope fans out per user, but its `homeManager` content is emitted class-locally on the host, where `homeManager` is inert — it never reaches the users' Home Manager evaluation (docs: `home-manager.mdx`, "Host-scope parametric aspects no longer deliver homeManager content to users"). So include it on each user's aspect tree:

```nix
den.aspects.<user>.includes = [ den.aspects.<feature> ];
# or host-qualified, for one host's user only:
den.aspects."<user>@<host>".includes = [ den.aspects.<feature> ];
```

If the aspect must live at host scope, route the home content through an explicit `to-users` policy, `provides`, or the host-aspects battery.

**Optional, if you want typed flags instead of freeform:** the Aspect Settings pattern (docs: `aspect-settings.mdx`) — declare `settings.graphics = lib.mkOption { type = types.bool; default = false; }` on the aspect, set `den.hosts.<system>.<name>.settings.<feature>.graphics = true;` per host, and read `host.settings.<feature>.graphics` in the aspect. For a shared capability vocabulary that many aspects read, plain freeform `capabilities` is the simpler idiom (docs `declare-hosts.mdx` shows exactly this shape for a freeform `gpu` attribute).

Precondition: the users must have `"homeManager"` in their `classes` (the default is `[ "user" ]`; `den.schema.user.classes = lib.mkDefault [ "homeManager" ]` if you haven't set it fleet-wide).","confidence":0.8,"gaps":["No repo/rev given, so I did not eval the asker's flake; verified the pattern against denful/den@main source (entities/host.nix, home-env.nix, batteries/home-manager.nix, options.nix) and its docs","Assumed host.capabilities is a freeform attribute set; if the asker's pinned den release predates the host-scope homeManager change, the leak behavior noted in home-manager.mdx may differ"],"escalate_forced":"no"}

