{"answer":"**In den, put the config on the host's aspect under `provides.to-users.homeManager`.**

`provides` keys are cross-entity routing: `provides.<name>` delivers to the host/user named `<name>`, `provides.to-hosts` to every host a user lives on, and **`provides.to-users` to every user on the host** (den docs, `guides/mutual.mdx`, sini/den@main). They are translated into cross-entity policy effects automatically — no battery or enable flag needed.

Concrete example, straight from the official `templates/default` template (`modules/igloo.nix`):

```nix
{
  # host aspect, named after the host
  den.aspects.igloo = {
    nixos =
      { pkgs, ... }:
      {
        environment.systemPackages = [ pkgs.hello ];
      };

    # provides a default home environment for ALL users on igloo
    provides.to-users.homeManager =
      { pkgs, ... }:
      {
        home.packages = [ pkgs.vim ];
      };
  };
}
```

The host's subtree spans every user on it, so this lands in each user's `homeManager` class, which den's home-env pipeline then forwards into `home-manager.users.<userName>` of that host's NixOS/Darwin config.

**Precondition — users must opt into the `homeManager` class** (docs, `guides/home-manager.mdx`):

```nix
# per user
den.hosts.x86_64-linux.igloo.users.tux.classes = [ "homeManager" ];

# or make it the default for all users unless they specify their own classes
den.schema.user.classes = lib.mkDefault [ "homeManager" ];
```

Home integration only activates when the host has a supported OS class (`nixos`/`darwin`) and at least one of its users carries `homeManager`.

**Variations**

- One specific user on that host: `den.aspects.igloo.provides.alice.homeManager.programs.vim.enable = true;`
- Conditional per user: `provides.to-users = { user, ... }: { homeManager.programs.helix.enable = user.name == "alice"; };`

**Two documented traps**

- A `provides` registered on a *user* aspect is subtree-scoped: it reaches that user and the hosts it lives on, but **never sibling users** on the same host. For "all users on one host" you must register it on the *host* aspect.
- A plain host-scope `{ user, ... }:` parametric aspect does **not** deliver `homeManager` content to the host's users anymore (the old "leak" was removed in the #609 binding rewrite — home content emitted at host scope is inert there). That's exactly why `provides.to-users` (or an explicit `to-users` policy) is the route for this.

