{"answer":"**Nobody in den sets `host.gpu` — it's your own data, set on the host declaration.**

The `host` argument an aspect receives *is* the resolved `den.hosts.<system>.<name>` config: the host entity is a submodule with a freeform type, so any attribute you set there lands on `host` in every aspect for that host. That is exactly the worked example in den's docs (`docs/src/content/docs/guides/declare-hosts.mdx`), which matches the aspect you quoted:

```nix
den.hosts.x86_64-linux.laptop = {
  users.alice = { };
  gpu = "nvidia";  # custom attribute, accessible in aspects via host.gpu
};

den.aspects.laptop.includes = [
  ({ host, ... }: lib.optionalAttrs (host ? gpu) {
    nixos.hardware.nvidia.enable = true;
  })
];
```

So: **who** — you (the user), in your flake; **where** — under `den.hosts.<system>.<name>`, wherever your `den.hosts` declaration lives (flake.nix or an imported module). `gpu` is not a den option: `nix/lib/entities/host.nix` defines only the schema options (`name`, `hostName`, `system`, `class`, `aspect`, `users`, `instantiate`, `intoAttr`, internal `mainModule`/`__resolveResult`/`__pathSetByScope`) and makes everything else freeform via `freeformType = deepMergeAttrs` (recursive merge). The `host` context arg itself is wired in `resolvedCtxModule` in `nix/lib/entities/_types.nix`, where the entity's own `config` is what flows into the aspect as `host`.

