{"answer":"Test for callability like this:

```nix
isCallable = v:
  builtins.isFunction v
  || (builtins.isAttrs v && ?v.__functor);
```

**Why `builtins.isFunction` says false.** In `NixOS/nix` master, `src/libexpr/primops.cc`, `prim_isFunction` is implemented as `v.mkBool(args[0]->type() == nFunction)` — it only recognizes `nFunction` values (lambdas, partially applied builtins). A functor is an attrset (`nAttrs`), so it reports `false` by design.

**How the evaluator calls it.** In `src/libexpr/eval.cc`, `EvalState::callFunction` has a dedicated branch:

```cpp
else if (vCur.type() == nAttrs && (functor = vCur.attrs()->get(s.functor))) {
    callFunction(*functor->value, std::to_array({&(*allocValue() = vCur), args[0]}), vCur, functor->pos);
    ...
}
```

i.e. `v x` on a functor `v` desugars to `v.__functor v x` — the attrset itself is passed as the first argument. The same file has the internal canonical check:

```cpp
bool EvalState::isFunctor(const Value & fun) const
{
    return fun.type() == nAttrs && fun.attrs()->get(s.functor);
}
```

**Caveats:**
- There is no `builtins.isCallable`; the `isAttrs && ?__functor` idiom above is the standard way (`?` already returns `false` on non-attrsets).
- Don't use `builtins.functionArgs` as a test: `prim_functionArgs` throws `'functionArgs' requires a function` for functors.
- The test only checks for the attribute's existence; a malformed functor (`__functor = 1`) passes it and fails at call time. If that matters, wrap a sentinel call in `builtins.tryEval`.

Verified against the `NixOS/nix` master source via raw fetch (`src/libexpr/eval.cc`, `src/libexpr/primops.cc`); no eval run was possible since no repo/rev was given to evaluate against.","confidence":0.9,"gaps":["No eval run: no repo/rev given to evaluate against, so the behaviour of isFunction on a functor was verified from source only, not reproduced with a computed value"],"escalate_forced":"no"}

{"answer":"Two supported routes (both verified in nixpkgs master, `lib/modules.nix`):

**1. `specialArgs` — for values needed when resolving module structure (e.g. `imports`):**
```nix
lib.evalModules {
  specialArgs = { myValue = "hello"; };
  modules = [
    { myValue, ... }: {          # destructure directly
      options.greeting = lib.mkOption { type = lib.types.str; default = "hi ${myValue}"; };
    };
  ];
}.config
```
`doCollect` builds each module's argument set as `{ inherit lib options specialArgs; …; config = …; } // specialArgs`, and `applyModuleArgs` resolves every name in a module function's pattern from that set — so any `specialArgs` key is a top-level module-function argument (also reachable as `specialArgs.myValue` / `config._module.specialArgs`).

**2. `config._module.args` — the source-recommended way for everything else:**
```nix
modules = [
  { config, ... }: { config._module.args = { myValue = "hello"; }; };
  { myValue, ... }: { options.greeting = lib.mkOption { type = lib.types.str; default = "hi ${myValue}"; }; };
];
```
`applyModuleArgs` falls back to `config._module.args.<name>` for destructured names not in the args set (the `config` there is the merged config — referencing it from `imports` is the documented infinite-recursion trap). The option is `types.lazyAttrsOf types.raw`, so values stay lazy. NixOS does exactly this: `modulesModule` in `nixos/lib/eval-config.nix` sets `config._module.args = { inherit noUserModules baseModules extraModules modules; }`.

**Caveats:** submodules don't inherit args from parents/siblings (exception: `name`); the old top-level `args` argument to `evalModules` is deprecated (warning: "Please set config._module.args instead"); and for file-based modules with per-instance arguments, use `lib.modules.importApply ./module.nix { bar = …; }` — the source warns that routing that through `specialArgs` creates incomplete modules.","confidence":0.9,"gaps":["eval and clone runners were refused in this evaluation; all claims verified by fetching nixpkgs master source (lib/modules.nix, nixos/lib/eval-config.nix) rather than by running a live eval demonstration","exact line numbers in lib/modules.nix not recorded; cited by definition name (evalModules, doCollect, applyModuleArgs, _module.args)"],"escalate_forced":"no"}

