{"answer":"In current nixpkgs, `enableACME = true` no longer needs a `services.nginx.acme` block: the nginx module wires up `security.acme.certs."example.org"` for you, and the failure you're hitting is one of the ACME module's assertions. Which thing you must set depends on the exact message:

1. **"You must accept the CA's terms of service … `security.acme.acceptTerms` to `true`"** (on nixpkgs ≤ 25.05 the option is `security.acme.acceptsTerms`) — the classic one. Set:
   ```nix
   security.acme.acceptTerms = true; # acceptsTerms on older releases
   ```

2. **"Exactly one of `security.acme.certs.example.org.dnsProvider`, `webroot`, `listenHTTP` and `s3Bucket` is required."** — you must tell the ACME client where the HTTP-01 challenge files go. The vhost's `acmeRoot` is passed to the cert as its webroot, so:
   ```nix
   services.nginx.virtualHosts."example.org" = {
     enableACME = true;
     acmeRoot = "/var/lib/letsencrypt";
   };
   ```
   (equivalently `security.acme.defaults.webroot = "/var/lib/letsencrypt";` — one of `webroot` / `listenHTTP` / `s3Bucket` / `dnsProvider`, not more than one).

3. **"…enableACME requires a HTTP listener to answer to ACME requests."** — your vhost's effective `listen` lines are all `proxyProtocol = true` (or none). Keep at least one plain, non-proxyProtocol HTTP listener on port 80 for that vhost; `forceSSL = true` is fine, `onlySSL` with a port-443-only `listen` is not.

4. **"Certificate example.org (group=…) must be readable by service(s) nginx (user=nginx groups=…)"** — the cert's group doesn't match what the nginx service runs as. The nginx module sets the cert's `group` to `services.nginx.group` (default `nginx`) by default; if you've overridden `security.acme.defaults.group` or the cert's `group`, set it to a group the nginx user belongs to (or the nginx group).

Paste the exact assertion text if it matches none of these — I checked all four ACME-related assertions in `nixos/modules/services/web-servers/nginx/nginx.nix` and `nixos/modules/security/acme/default.nix` (plus `mk-cert-ownership-assertion.nix`) and those are the only ones that fire for `enableACME = true`.

How I know: I read these assertions and messages directly from the nixpkgs source at `NixOS/nixpkgs@master` (`nixos/modules/services/web-servers/nginx/nginx.nix`, `nixos/modules/security/acme/default.nix`).","confidence":0.8,"gaps":["Read assertions from nixpkgs master; user's nixpkgs rev/channel unknown — exact option name differs on older releases (acceptTerms vs acceptsTerms), the message will say which","Could not eval the user's flake (no repo/rev provided), so I did not reproduce the specific assertion the user hit"],"escalate_forced":"no"}

{"answer":"Set the sshd option in your NixOS config:

```nix
services.openssh.settings.PermitRootLogin = "no";
```

e.g. in context:

```nix
services.openssh = {
  enable = true;
  openFirewall = true;
  settings = {
    PermitRootLogin = "no";
    # optional hardening:
    PasswordAuthentication = false;
    KbdInteractiveAuthentication = false;
  };
};
```

then `nixos-rebuild switch`.

**Why this is the right knob:** in the nixpkgs module `nixos/modules/services/networking/ssh/sshd.nix` (master), `PermitRootLogin` is defined with `default = "prohibit-password"` and type `nullOr (enum ["yes" "without-password" "prohibit-password" "forced-commands-only" "no"])`, described as "Whether the root user can login using ssh." The default `"prohibit-password"` is why root key-based logins work out of the box; `"no"` forbids root SSH logins entirely. Note the legacy top-level `services.openssh.permitRootLogin` is just a renamed alias of `settings.PermitRootLogin` (deprecated — prefer the settings form), and setting `PermitRootLogin = null` omits the directive from the generated `sshd_config`, falling back to OpenSSH's built-in default. The official NixOS wiki SSH page recommends the same `PermitRootLogin = "no"` setting.

**Caveat:** you lose root over SSH — keep a regular sudo user as your access path and have console access available when you first apply this.","confidence":0.9,"gaps":["did not evaluate a concrete system configuration (no repo/rev provided), so the final rendered sshd_config on the asker's machine is unverified"],"escalate_forced":"no"}

